  "title": "Bigger Than, and How Long",
  "drivablePrefix": 3,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:982fe8e39d02cf48",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "LA-C288-maior-quam",
      "sequence": 15670,
      "title": "longior quam — bigger than, faster than",
      "headword": "longior quam; maior, minor, melior",
      "romanization": "longior quam; maior, minor, melior",
      "gloss": "comparing two things — -ior on the adjective, quam for 'than', and the three comparatives you learn whole",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ec6e1dab7e489192",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"long\", then \"fast\". (Longus. Celer.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An experience is often a comparison: the road was longer than you thought, the ship faster than the cart."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: -ior and quam",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take the stem of the adjective and add -ior, \"more\": long-us, longior, longer. quam is \"than\":"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nāvis celerior est quam raeda. — A ship is faster than a carriage."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "For a neuter word the ending is -ius:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mare altius est quam flūmen. — The sea is deeper than the river. Iter longius erat quam putāvī. — The journey was longer than I thought."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mare and iter are neuter, so altius, longius. Nāvis is feminine, and -ior serves a man or a woman alike: celerior."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Facilis gives facilior, easier. An adjective with a vowel before -us, like idōneus, takes magis, \"more\", in front instead: magis idōneus quam, more suitable than."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: maior, minor, melior",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three common adjectives have a comparative of their own, learned whole:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "magnus — maior, bigger. parvus — minor, smaller. bonus, good — melior, better."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Rōma maior est quam oppidum meum. — Rome is bigger than my town."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: maior and melior",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The -ior ending is inherited: EtymDB 2.1 builds maior from the root of magnus, Proto-Indo-European megh2-, \"great\", with a comparative ending -yos. melior is traced to a Proto-Indo-European melyos, \"better\", with no link to bonus at all, which is why it has to be learned whole. English took major, minor and, through French, ameliorate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "longus, longior; celer, celerior; facilis, facilior",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *longus, longior*; *celer, celerior*; *facilis, facilior*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Nāvis celerior est quam raeda.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Nāvis celerior est quam raeda.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Mare altius est quam flūmen.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Mare altius est quam flūmen.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "maior, minor, melior",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *maior, minor, melior*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Rōma maior est quam oppidum meum.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Rōma maior est quam oppidum meum.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "two things you know, compared with quam",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: two things you know, compared with *quam*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"longer\". (Longior.) Say \"than\". (Quam.) Say \"bigger\", \"smaller\", \"better\". (Maior, minor, melior.) Why is it altius with mare? (Mare is neuter.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "LA-C288-abhinc",
      "sequence": 15680,
      "title": "abhinc — ago, and for how long",
      "headword": "abhinc",
      "romanization": "abhinc",
      "gloss": "ago; counting back from now",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:63a831ef3ca4e8ad",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"for a long time\". (Diū.) Then \"a year\". (Annus.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Diū says long without saying how long. Latin can count it out, and the case it counts in is the accusative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the accusative of how long",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Trēs annōs Rōmae habitāvī. — I lived in Rome for three years. Duās hōrās ambulāvimus. — We walked for two hours."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No word for \"for\": the stretch of time stands in the accusative, annōs, hōrās, and the verb says what filled it. Duās is duo agreeing with hōrās."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: abhinc",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "abhinc — \"ago\". It counts back from now, and the count goes in the accusative too:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Abhinc trēs annōs Rōmam vēnī. — I came to Rome three years ago. Abhinc decem diēs nāvis solvit. — The ship sailed ten days ago."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put the two together and a life has a shape: abhinc for when it began, the accusative for how long it lasted. Cicero uses abhinc with the accusative in his speeches and letters (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: abhinc",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "abhinc is built from ab, \"from\", and hinc, \"from here, from this point\" (EtymDB 2.1), and hinc in turn comes from hīc, \"here\". So the word is \"from this point\", counted backwards into the past. Lewis & Short note that some early writers also used it forwards, \"from now on\", a sense that did not last."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "abhinc",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *abhinc*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Trēs annōs Rōmae habitāvī.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Trēs annōs Rōmae habitāvī.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Abhinc trēs annōs Rōmam vēnī.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Abhinc trēs annōs Rōmam vēnī.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Duās hōrās ambulāvimus; iter longius erat quam putāvī.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Duās hōrās ambulāvimus; iter longius erat quam putāvī.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "how long you have lived where you live, and when you came",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: how long you have lived where you live, and when you came]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does abhinc mean? (Ago.) Say \"three years ago\". (Abhinc trēs annōs.) Say \"for two hours\". (Duās hōrās.) Which case counts the time? (The accusative.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "LA-R288-bigger-and-how-long",
      "sequence": 15690,
      "title": "Review — what it was like",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "describing an experience — how it felt, what was bigger or better, how long it lasted and how long ago",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:176eb32770ad774b",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"the sea is deeper than the river\". (Mare altius est quam flūmen.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two chapters, and an experience can be told whole. How it felt, with eram and quod. What you hope for, with spērō and cupiō. What was bigger, faster or better, with -ior, quam, maior, minor and melior. And time counted out: the accusative for how long, abhinc for how long ago."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Abhinc duōs annōs Rōmam vēnī. Decem diēs ibi mānsī. Rōma maior erat quam putāvī, et laetus eram. — I came to Rome two years ago. I stayed there ten days. Rome was bigger than I thought, and I was happy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
